 "method": "Sum of the 'No health insurance coverage' cells in B27001 (18 sex-by-age cells from 2009 on; three age cells in the 2008 release) divided by the civilian noninstitutionalized population (B27001_001), times 100. The sum's margin of error is the root-sum-of-squares of the published cell margins, which slightly overstates the true margin; the share uses the formula for proportions. Race breakdowns use the collapsed iterated tables C27001B/D/H/I the same way (three age groups). Citywide figures are for New York city (place 3651000); peers are matched on place GEOID (state + place FIPS) from data/peers.json; borough figures are the five counties.",
 "caveats": [
  "The ACS health insurance question starts in 2008; there are no earlier values.",
  "The ACS counts people with only Indian Health Service coverage as uninsured.",
  "Race groups B (Black) and D (Asian) are race alone and include Hispanic members of those races; H is white alone, not Hispanic; I is Hispanic or Latino of any race.",
  "The ACS 1-year 2020 standard tables were never released (the Census Bureau published only experimental estimates because of pandemic data-collection problems), so 2020 is missing from every ACS series.",
  "The 2005 ACS covered only people in households; group quarters (dorms, prisons, nursing homes, shelters) were added in 2006. 2005 values are not strictly comparable to later years.",
  "All ACS figures are survey estimates. moe is the 90 percent margin of error, published by the Census Bureau for single cells and derived with the Bureau's approximation formulas for sums, shares and ratios.",
  "Margins of error: for 2010, 2011, 2012, 2013, 2014, 2015, 2016, 2017, 2018, 2019, 2021, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2025 the moe is the one the Census Bureau publishes in subject table S2701 (which matches this estimate to its published rounding) for the city, the 2025 peers and the boroughs. Other years, and the race breakdowns, use the derived margin described in the method."
 ]
}
